/* The manifest, as resource 1 (CREATEPROCESS_MANIFEST_RESOURCE_ID). An exe
   with no RT_MANIFEST resource gets comctl32 v5 for every standard control;
   see polter.manifest for what that looked like. */
#include <windows.h>
1 RT_MANIFEST "polter.manifest"

/* The application's own name, and it was missing entirely.

   **Measured, not inferred**: the shipped `polter-host.exe` contained no
   `VS_VERSION_INFO` resource at all -- searching the binary for that
   signature, for `FileDescription` and for `ProductName` as UTF-16 gave zero
   hits each, while `Polter` (the window class names and friends) gave thirty.

   **So this exe had no friendly name to give, and every place Windows asks
   for one fell back to the file name.** That is one gap with several exits:
   the notification's attribution line (which is where somebody noticed it,
   task 294), Task Manager's "Name" column, the taskbar's hover tooltip, the
   file properties dialog, and the UAC prompt. Fixing it in the notification
   code would have fixed one exit out of five, and `windows/AGENTS.md`'s
   "Names" section already says which way this must go: **user-visible strings
   are Polter; internal artefacts keep their own names.**

   ⚠️ **The mechanism is reasoned, not measured, and the difference matters
   for whoever checks this.** The claim is that Windows takes the friendly
   application name from `FileDescription`. It cannot be established from the
   machine this was written on. If the notification still says
   `polter-host.exe` after this, look at Task Manager and the file properties
   before concluding this change was wrong: those two changing while the
   notification does not means the attribution line goes through the
   AppUserModelID instead, which is a different piece of work and deliberately
   not attempted here -- an AUMID that no shortcut or registry entry resolves
   fails by doing nothing, which is the outcome `notify.rs`'s header already
   names as the worst of the three.

   **`FILEVERSION` is 0,0,0,0 on purpose, and that is the whole reason it is
   written out rather than omitted.** Omitting it produces the same zeros --
   the field defaults -- but as an accident, and the file properties dialog
   then shows a number nobody decided on. There is no trustworthy build-time
   version to put here: `Cargo.toml` says 0.1.0 while the product is v0.6 and
   nothing keeps those two in step, and the version that is *real* is the
   core's (`main.rs`'s `core_commit` parses `1.3.2-dev+<commit>` out of
   libghostty at run time), which a static resource cannot hold. Inventing a
   third number would be a third reader of a fact that already has two
   disagreeing ones.

   **`CompanyName` and `LegalCopyright` are absent for the same reason.** An
   empty field shows nothing; a made-up one is exactly the defect this file is
   fixing, one line further down the same dialog.

   **One `.rc` serves two binaries** -- `polter-host.exe` (GUI) and
   `polter-cli.exe` (console), one crate, see `Cargo.toml`. `FileDescription`
   is true of both. `OriginalFilename` can only name one, and it names the GUI
   binary because that is the one a person sees in Task Manager; on
   `polter-cli.exe` that field is therefore wrong, which is why it is said here
   rather than left for somebody to find. */
VS_VERSION_INFO VERSIONINFO
FILEVERSION 0,0,0,0
PRODUCTVERSION 0,0,0,0
FILEFLAGSMASK VS_FFI_FILEFLAGSMASK
FILEFLAGS 0
FILEOS VOS_NT_WINDOWS32
FILETYPE VFT_APP
FILESUBTYPE VFT2_UNKNOWN
BEGIN
    BLOCK "StringFileInfo"
    BEGIN
        /* 0409 = en-US, 04B0 = Unicode. The pair is repeated in the
           `Translation` value below; the two must agree or the shell reads
           the block for a language the file does not claim. */
        BLOCK "040904B0"
        BEGIN
            VALUE "FileDescription", "Polter"
            VALUE "ProductName",     "Polter"
            VALUE "InternalName",    "polter-host"
            VALUE "OriginalFilename","polter-host.exe"
        END
    END
    BLOCK "VarFileInfo"
    BEGIN
        VALUE "Translation", 0x0409, 0x04B0
    END
END
